  "body_html": "<h1>Value In Finite Zero-Sum Perfect-Information Games</h1>\n<p>Every finite zero-sum perfect-information game, with or without Nature, has a\nvalue and both players have pure optimal strategies.</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Sketch</p>\n<p>Use forward induction on the tree. If Nature moves at the root, the value is the\nprobability-weighted average of the successor subgame values. If player 1 moves,\nthe value is the maximum of successor values; if player 2 moves, it is the minimum.\nOptimal pure strategies are obtained by choosing an optimal successor and then\nfollowing optimal strategies in the selected subgame.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Proposition 6.2.5] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MechDesign.pdf</code>. Every finite zero-sum perfect-information game with or without Nature has a value and pure optimal strategies.</li>\n</ul>",
